<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand de Lesseps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand de Lesseps was a French diplomat and entrepreneur who developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, opened in 1869. Encouraged by that success, he launched a French company to build a sea-level canal across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> in 1881, but disease, landslides and engineering problems overwhelmed the project, which collapsed in 1889 after many thousands of workers had died. The bankruptcy caused a great political scandal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in which he was convicted, though the sentence was later quashed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-de-lesseps.jpg" alt="Ferdinand de Lesseps" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinand_de_Lesseps_by_Nadar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand de Lesseps was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 19 November 1805 into a family of diplomats; his father Mathieu de Lesseps was in the consular service. Through his father he came to know Muhammad Ali, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Muhammad Ali's son Sa'id, who became his friend. His own diplomatic career began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in 1825 and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, Málaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. In Alexandria he read a memoir on the abandoned ancient canal at Suez, which helped give him the idea of a canal across the isthmus. Recalled in 1849 after a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he left the diplomatic service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Sa'id Pasha became viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1854, Lesseps landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 7 November, and on 30 November the viceroy signed a concession authorising him to oversee the French portion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>'s construction. British opposition, including that of Lord Palmerston, did not deter him. French subscribers supplied more than half of the capital of two hundred million francs, and the Egyptian government took shares worth eighty million francs. The first blow of the pickaxe was struck at Port Said on 25 April 1859, and the canal was opened on 17 November 1869 by the Khedive Ismail Pasha.</p><p>A congress of 136 delegates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in May 1879 voted for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> canal without locks, like Suez, and Lesseps, aged 74, became president of the company. Work began in 1882 but was dogged by landslides and by deaths from malaria and yellow fever. The Panama Canal Company declared itself bankrupt in December 1888 and entered liquidation in February 1889.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The failure became known as the Panama Canal Scandal after it emerged by 1892 that 150 French deputies had been bribed to vote the company financial aid. In February 1893 Lesseps, his son Charles and others were tried and found guilty. Lesseps was fined and sentenced to prison, but the Court of Cassation overturned the prison sentence because more than three years had passed since the offence. In 1904 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> bought the company's assets and resumed work on a revised plan. Lesseps died in Indre on 7 December 1894 and was buried in Père Lachaise Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand de Lesseps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…lay in transit: the Panama Railroad, built by American investors in 1850–1855, carried gold seekers bound for California, and a French company led by <b>Ferdinand de Lesseps</b> began a canal in 1881 before collapsing in 1889, after perhaps 20,000 workers had died, mostly of yellow fever and malaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 1860s he had proposed to Isma'il Pasha, Khedive of Egypt, a huge lighthouse in the form of a robed peasant woman holding a torch at the entrance to the Suez Canal; the khedive and <b>Ferdinand de Lesseps</b> declined it because of the cost.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ferdinand de Lesseps</b> struck the first blow at the site of Port Said.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-25">25 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Versailles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Guilly, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Versailles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4599 459 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand de Lesseps"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5049 729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5049 729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.81,2.14&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
